### 2. Handle Non-Integer Inputs

If you're expecting non-integer inputs and want to handle them gracefully, you can use a loop to keep prompting until a valid input is given.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        while (!scanner.hasNextInt()) { // Loop until a valid integer is provided
            System.out.print("Please enter a number: ");
            String input = scanner.nextLine();
            try {
                int number = Integer.parseInt(input);
                break; // Exit the loop once a valid integer is received
            } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
                System.out.println("Invalid input! Please enter a valid integer.");
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Number entered: " + number);
    }
}
```

### 3. Use Try-Catch Blocks Thoroughly

Make sure to wrap any code that reads input within a `try-catch` block to handle exceptions properly.

```java
